[And before you say it, no... none, or at least very little, of this is a new addition to the theory; it's just a crystallization of concepts that we've been using interchangeably for two years--or rather, most of this is just explaining what is meant by the terms; some of it is drawing distinctions that we habitually collapse]

Your choice of illustrative images is--I was going to say... odd--but no. The images do not illustrate the concepts as defined. I know it's extra steps to 'look' at a picture and decide whether it does or does not represent the concepts we're discussing, but the thing is, if you use pictures blindly, then you aren't actually aware of what you're saying. It's like if I were to construct my prompts out of newspaper clippings without reading them, assuming whatever articles I clipped from are saying what I intend to say because the headlines suggest they're in the same neighborhood. Meanwhile, I've got "clarifying" parantheticals suggest what I'm trying to say involves bylines and mastheads and advertisements and parts of the story that are the opposite of the part I'm talking about when I cite it.&#x20;

In this case, you've illustrated I. Surface ripple with a... I mean that *could* be an extreme surface ripple on a *ring* (not on a helix), you've got II. Propagating structural loop illustrated by a persistent helical coil, and III. Persistent structural coil illustrated by... I don't know what that is... three tubes constructed out of concentric cylenders, one of which has a bunch of chaotic string wrapped around and through it.&#x20;

Approximate geometry isn't geometry. It's unsystematic.

